    "body": "## Design note — public-domain storage normalization (from issue #6009, task 12998)\n\nRecorded by the repo queue worker (lane code-m, task 12998) as the linked design note the #6009 packet specifies: the #5977 taxonomy design record does not exist in the repo yet, so this note — posted identically on issue #5977 and issue #6009 and linked both ways — is the record the #5977 taxonomy spec must adopt before it finalizes. Issue #6009: https://github.com/developing-today/code/issues/6009 · Issue #5977: https://github.com/developing-today/code/issues/5977\n\n### The rule\n\n1. **Storage normalization.** When a user selects \"public domain\" for a work, the stored license value is the normalized SPDX identifier — `CC0-1.0` for content, `0BSD` (or Unlicense) for code. A bare \"public domain\" string is never stored as a license value.\n2. **Registry / checker visibility.** The registry and the combination checker see only the normalized SPDX identifier (`CC0-1.0` / `0BSD` / Unlicense). The user's original \"public domain\" selection may be kept as provenance metadata, but no downstream consumer ever has to interpret a bare dedication.\n3. **Taxonomy unchanged.** This is storage normalization only: #5977 keeps \"public domain\" and CC0 as separate taxonomy entries, exactly as its scope states.\n\n### UI copy (one sentence)\n\n> \"Because a bare public-domain dedication isn't legally portable everywhere, we'll store your choice as CC0-1.0 for content (or 0BSD for code) — the closest portable equivalent — noting that CC0 doesn't cover patent or trademark rights.\"\n\n### Why (the caveats, carried honestly)\n\n\"Public domain\" is not a portable legal act: in Germany's monistic author's-rights system copyright can neither be transferred nor waived, so a bare dedication fails as drafted; several other jurisdictions lack a dedication concept or treat it as revocable. CC0 is engineered around this (waiver, then a fallback permissive license, then a covenant not to assert) — but CC0 expressly does *not* license patent or trademark rights, which is why it is not used here for code (Fedora reclassified CC0 as not-allowed for code in 2022; content/data remain fine) and why code normalizes to `0BSD` (or Unlicense) instead. Sources are on issue #6009.\n\n<!-- agent-note:v1 agent=repo-worker-code lane=code-m state=design_note task=12998 -->\n",
